+### Complete release duties and historical reachability — local TCA1 implementation
+
+`CellReleaseRetentionStoreV1` composes the original physical retention owner with
+a fresh `TCA1` catalog. The catalog stores canonical `CellModelReleaseRootV1`
+bytes, including the complete unchanged TCD1 dependency graph, explicit router
+commitment and direct lineage identities. It does not create another payload CAS
+or change the old arena's schema. The embedded owner remains responsible for
+physical allocation, complete payload hashes, irreversible copy/repair charges
+and successor readback. The wrapper never exposes a mutable embedded owner.
+
+Every immutable `CellReleaseRetentionContractV1` binds its original operation and
+funding identities, responsible owner, declared failure domain, complete-replica
+slot and required replica count, lease interval, last permitted repair height,
+complete model root and exact per-object physical contracts. Objects are in
+canonical manifest-ID order and must cover every closure file exactly. Each
+physical contract must cover the release's whole lease and allow at least one
+complete repair. The complete contract is bounded by 4 MiB, and the configured
+catalog maximum is at most 128 roots, including retired history. The
+`trnm.cell-release-retention-contract.v1` commitment also binds the physical
+store identity. These fields identify admitted terms; they do not authenticate
+funding or establish independent replicas by themselves.
+
+| Actual interface | Effect and refusal boundary |
+| --- | --- |
+| `reserve_release` | Persist the complete immutable root intent before any per-object physical reservation. Partial capacity or IO failure leaves a pending root protecting all declared dependencies. An exact retry resumes the same reservations. |
+| `acknowledge_release` | Use the original initial-copy operation for every member, then freshly verify every physical target before sealing the release ACK. Any failed or unknown member leaves the release pending. |
+| `contract` / `reconcile_release` | Recover the exact stored terms and finish a lost release ACK using only fresh target readback. The reconciliation API has no source/cache parameter and cannot redispatch a copy. |
+| `verify_release` / `populate_cache` | Hash actual retained payloads under one absolute deadline; optionally rehydrate the original disposable chunk cache for the existing peer server. An old ACK is not fresh availability. |
+| `repair_member` | Require exact dependency membership, the original immutable repair-height bound and a separately admitted repair identity; retain the original byte charges, cooperative checkpoint and target-only recovery semantics. Missing the deadline does not erase the obligation. |
+| `reconcile_repair_member` | Resolve the original repair journal entry by target-only readback, including after its dispatch-height deadline. The API has no source/cache parameter and cannot start a new write. |
+| `retire_release` | Require explicit expiry, a previously acknowledged release and fresh complete target verification. A pending, unknown or corrupt duty cannot be removed from reachability. This changes no physical slot. |
+| `retire_object` | Validate the complete catalog and hold its writer transaction through physical retirement. Every nonretired root, including expired or partially reserved roots, blocks reclamation of its referenced object. Original per-object expiry and actual successor checks still apply. |
+
+Both release retirement and the original per-object retirement now refuse an
+unsettled repair journal entry, even when its fsynced payload is already healthy.
+Fresh byte readback alone cannot erase an unknown completion. Reconcile that
+exact repair identity from its target first; this preserves its original charge
+and adds no second copy. Root retirement is refused before changing reachability,
+so the target-only repair remains accessible through the original live duty.
+
+Two live historical releases sharing a manifest must reference the same original
+physical operation and exactly the same immutable physical terms. Retiring one
+root therefore cannot reclaim that shared copy. Once all references have been
+explicitly retired, the original physical owner may reclaim the slot after its
+own lease expires. A later release may retain the same content under new funded
+operations; old root and operation history remains immutable. The catalog never
+infers a promise to retain every ancestor's payload merely from a lineage hash:
+each version whose bytes are promised needs its own explicit duty.
+
+The catalog uses a distinct application ID and exact STRICT schema with bounded
+canonical records and checksums. Reopen does not create a missing catalog,
+migrate old schemas, expire roots or reset history. An unexpected trigger, changed
+contract, inconsistent status, unknown dependency or incomplete catalog refuses
+retirement. Checksums detect inconsistent local state, not authenticated operator
+honesty or complete-host rollback. The existing exclusive arena lock and trusted
+local owner boundary remain required.
+
+This closes a local release-wide storage/reachability software slice. The new
+wrapper is not yet composed with the Node's original funded release operation;
+its funding identities and supplied heights must still come from that owner.
+The replica count and failure domain are declarations, not measurements of other
+hosts. Automatic repair detection, independently operated replicas, continuous
+byte-time metering, tariff/renewal policy, external rollback protection and the
+PN5 campaign remain unqualified. No successful local ACK grants CURRENT,
+FinalUse, model quality, a monetary payment or production activation.
+
+### PN4/PN5 funded release-wide and independent custody interface — remaining target
